<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thagaste</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thagaste was a Roman town in Numidia, on the site of modern Souk Ahras in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. It is best known as the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> in 354, who returned there after his conversion to found a religious community before becoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Hippo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thagaste.jpg" alt="Thagaste" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mileve_Hippo_Thagaste_in_Numidia_(83193756).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thagaste, also written Tagaste, lay in the north-eastern highlands of Numidia, around 60 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>, 20 miles southwest of Thubursicum and about 150 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. The region was densely forested, and in antiquity its mountains served as a natural citadel against invaders, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. Researchers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> have linked the name to Tegueste, which they derive from a Guanche word of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> origin meaning humid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Thagaste began as a small Numidian village of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe. It became a Roman municipium in the first century of Roman rule and is mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>; a few Italian settlers lived there, but most inhabitants were romanised Berbers. Trade grew, and the town flourished above all under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>. Augustine's mother, Saint Monica, was a Christian, and his father, Patricius, was a pagan who later became a Christian.</p><p>The town had a basilica and a diocese, the most important in Byzantine Numidia; three of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> are known, Saint Firminus, Saint Alypius, Augustine's friend, and Saint Gennarus. The wealthy gens Valeria, later under Saint Melania, held an estate nearby so large that it included two episcopal sees, one Catholic and one Donatist. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> fortified Thagaste with walls, and it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> towards the end of the seventh century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After centuries of neglect French colonists rebuilt the town, now called Souk Ahras. By tradition Augustine meditated under an olive tree on a hill of the town; the tree still stands and is a meeting place for followers of Augustinian spirituality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thagaste” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Numidia was the stronghold of the Donatist church and the birthplace of Augustine at <b>Thagaste</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-numidia">Roman Numidia</a></li></ul></section>
